From: OCCTSwift ecosystem <noreply@secondmouse.au>
Subject: [PATCH] Application Framework - binary attribute drivers check a declared span against
 the data left before they allocate for it

The binary Paste of an array or list attribute reads a first and a last index, then allocates
last - first + 1 elements, and only then reads the elements (and, for the numeric arrays,
fails if the data ran out). Nothing relates the span to the record. A record of a few dozen
bytes declaring 2^30 elements asks Standard::Allocate for 1 to 17 GiB:

   IntegerArray / RealArray / ReferenceArray   Init(first, last)                 4 to 8 GiB
   ByteArray / BooleanArray / the *List drivers a temporary NCollection_Array1   1 to 8 GiB
   ExtStringArray                              Init(first, last) of 16 byte strings, 17 GiB
   NamedData, DimTol, NoteBinData, GraphNode,
   Scope, VisMaterial                          an HArray1 or buffer by a declared size

A 64-bit process absorbs the request until a driver touches the pages (a ByteArray copy loop of
2 GiB took 19 s, an ExtStringArray constructor loop 26 s). A 32-bit address space (wasm) cannot
hold it, and a failed allocation there is the failure Standard_MMgrRaw reports for every later
allocation of the process.

Each driver now checks the declared count against the data left in the record with
BinObjMgt_Persistent::HasItems (previous patch), using the smallest size one element can take in
the record (an ExtendedString and an AsciiString take at least their terminator, a Boolean array
is one bit per element), and returns false, which BinLDrivers_DocumentRetrievalDriver reports as
a "failure reading attribute" warning and goes on. The check divides, so the count times the item
size is never formed: nothing overflows a 32-bit size_t.

Measured on the macOS slice of v4.0.0-kernel.7 with crafted files (Scripts/repro/3489 in
SecondMouseAU/OCCTSwift): the declared-span cases that requested 1 to 17 GiB request nothing; a
document saved with one attribute of every kind above and reopened is unchanged.

Tracked as SecondMouseAU/OCCTSwift#3489, follow-up to #3486 and #3477.

---
diff --git a/src/ApplicationFramework/TKBinL/BinMDataStd/BinMDataStd_RealListDriver.cxx b/src/ApplicationFramework/TKBinL/BinMDataStd/BinMDataStd_RealListDriver.cxx
--- a/src/ApplicationFramework/TKBinL/BinMDataStd/BinMDataStd_RealListDriver.cxx
+++ b/src/ApplicationFramework/TKBinL/BinMDataStd/BinMDataStd_RealListDriver.cxx
@@ -59,6 +59,10 @@
     const int aLength = aLastInd - aFirstInd + 1;
     if (aLength > 0)
     {
+      if (!theSource.HasItems(aLength, (int)sizeof(double)))
+      {
+        return false;
+      }
       NCollection_Array1<double> aTargetArray(aFirstInd, aLastInd);
       theSource.GetRealArray(&aTargetArray(aFirstInd), aLength);
       for (aIndex = aFirstInd; aIndex <= aLastInd; aIndex++)
